course: books
lesson: 15
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter VIII, part 1: The Queen's Croquet-Ground (painting the roses red)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Just inside the garden, three gardeners shaped like playing cards are painting white roses red. They squabble and blame each other, and one of them, Two, explains to Alice in a whisper: they planted a white rose-tree by mistake instead of a red one, and if the Queen finds out, they will all lose their heads.
explain: Then «The Queen! The Queen!» — the gardeners throw themselves flat on their faces, and a grand procession marches in: ten soldiers with clubs, ten courtiers with diamonds, ten royal children with hearts, the guests (the White Rabbit among them), the Knave of Hearts carrying the crown on a cushion, and last of all the King and Queen of Hearts.
explain: Alice stays standing, because what is the use of a procession if everyone lies on their faces? The Queen asks her name; Alice answers very politely, but thinks to herself, «they're only a pack of cards, after all». When the Queen screams «Off with her head!», Alice says «Nonsense!» loudly, and the Queen goes quiet.
explain: The Queen sees the half-painted roses and orders the gardeners' heads off. Alice quickly hides them in a big flower-pot; the soldiers can't find them and simply report «Their heads are gone». The Queen asks Alice if she can play croquet: «Yes!»
explain: Walking along, the nervous White Rabbit whispers that the Duchess is under sentence of execution for boxing the Queen's ears, and Alice gives a little scream of laughter. Then the game begins, and it is the strangest croquet ever: live hedgehogs for balls, live flamingoes for mallets, and soldiers bent double for the arches.
fact: The garden has a large rose-tree with white roses, and three gardeners are «busily painting them red».
fact: The gardeners are called Two, Five and Seven, like the numbers on playing cards.
fact: Five splashes paint on Two and blames Seven, who «jogged my elbow»; Seven answers «Always lay the blame on others!»
fact: Five says the Queen wants Seven beheaded for «bringing the cook tulip-roots instead of onions».
fact: Two explains: «this here ought to have been a red rose-tree, and we put a white one in by mistake»; «afore she comes» is old country talk for before she comes.
fact: The procession: ten soldiers carrying clubs, ten courtiers ornamented with diamonds, ten royal children ornamented with hearts, «jumping merrily along hand in hand, in couples», the guests, the Knave of Hearts with the King's crown on a crimson velvet cushion, and last the King and Queen of Hearts.
fact: Alice does not lie down, because «what would be the use of a procession» if nobody could see it?
fact: «My name is Alice, so please your Majesty» is the old polite way to speak to a queen; to herself she says «they're only a pack of cards, after all. I needn't be afraid of them!»
fact: The gardeners lie face down, and their backs have the same pattern as the rest of the pack, so the Queen can't tell who they are.
fact: Alice answers «How should I know? It's no business of mine», «surprised at her own courage»; the Queen turns crimson and screams «Off with her head!»; Alice says «Nonsense!» and the Queen is silent.
fact: The King says timidly «Consider, my dear: she is only a child!»; the Knave turns the gardeners over «very carefully, with one foot».
fact: Told to get up, the gardeners keep bowing to everybody, and the Queen screams «Leave off that! You make me giddy.»
fact: The Queen orders «Off with their heads!», and the procession moves on, with three of the soldiers staying behind to execute the gardeners; Alice says «You shan't be beheaded!» (shan't = shall not) and hides them in a large flower-pot.
fact: The soldiers shout «Their heads are gone, if it please your Majesty!» — true words that hide the truth: the heads are gone from sight, not cut off.
fact: The White Rabbit whispers that the Duchess is «under sentence of execution»; Alice asks «What for?», and when the Rabbit asks «Did you say 'What a pity!'?», she says she doesn't think it's at all a pity; the reason is «She boxed the Queen's ears» (boxed someone's ears = hit them on the side of the head), and Alice gives a little scream of laughter, so the Rabbit whispers «Oh, hush! The Queen will hear you!»
fact: The croquet-ground is «all ridges and furrows» (bumps and dips); the balls are live hedgehogs, the mallets live flamingoes, and the arches are soldiers bent double.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «lush» (Wales) = lovely; «proper job» (West Country) = well done; «it's giving» = it looks like, it's showing off; «yeah nah» (Australia, NZ) = no.
exercise: What colour are the roses on the rose-tree, and what are the gardeners doing to them? | They are white, and the gardeners are painting them red | They are red, and the gardeners are painting them white for the Queen's party | They are pink, and the gardeners are cutting them | They are yellow, and the gardeners are watering them
exercise: What are the gardeners' names? | Two, Five and Seven | Pat, Bill and Mary Ann | Three, Four and Nine | Elsie, Lacie and Tillie
exercise: Five splashes paint over Two. Whom does he blame? | Seven, who jogged his elbow | Two himself, for standing too close to the wet roses | The Queen, for rushing them | Alice, for watching him work
exercise: «That's right, Five! Always lay the blame on others!» Who says this? | Seven | Gardener Two | The Queen | The Knave
exercise: Why, according to Five, does the Queen want Seven beheaded? | For bringing the cook tulip-roots instead of onions | For painting the Queen's golden crown bright red by mistake last week | For stealing the tarts | For falling asleep at the concert
exercise: Why are the gardeners painting the roses? | They planted a white rose-tree by mistake, and it should have been red | The Queen asked for pink roses for the big croquet game this afternoon in the garden | Red paint keeps away hedgehogs | The white roses were dirty
exercise: What do the gardeners do when Five calls «The Queen! The Queen!»? | Throw themselves flat on their faces | Run off and hide behind the rose-tree with their brushes | Start singing a song | Hand Alice a brush
exercise: Who comes first in the procession? | Ten soldiers carrying clubs | The King and Queen of Hearts | The Knave carrying the crown | The royal children, hand in hand
exercise: How are the courtiers ornamented? | With diamonds | With hearts | With clubs | With spades
exercise: How do the ten royal children come along? | Jumping merrily along hand in hand, in couples, ornamented with hearts | Marching stiffly one by one behind the soldiers, carrying long spears and shields | Riding on flamingoes | Asleep on cushions carried by courtiers
exercise: Which guest does Alice recognise in the procession? | The White Rabbit | The Hatter | The Caterpillar | The Mock Turtle
exercise: What does the Knave of Hearts carry? | The King's crown on a crimson velvet cushion | A dish of tarts | A big trumpet and a scroll of parchment with the rules | The Queen's flamingo
exercise: Who comes last of all in the procession? | The King and Queen of Hearts | The Knave of Hearts | The White Rabbit | Ten soldiers
exercise: Why does Alice not lie down on her face like the gardeners? | What would be the use of a procession if nobody could see it? | She was too big to lie down in the narrow path between the rose-beds | The Cheshire Cat told her not to | She didn't see it coming
exercise: «My name is Alice, so please your Majesty.» What does Alice think to herself straight after? | «They're only a pack of cards, after all» | «I must lie down at once, or she will be cross» | «What a beautiful Queen she is» | «I wish Dinah were here»
exercise: Why can't the Queen tell who the three gardeners are? | They are lying face down, and their backs look like the rest of the pack | They have covered themselves all over in red paint from the top of the head to the toes | It is too dark | They are wearing masks
exercise: «How should I know? It's no business of mine.» How does Alice feel as she says this? | Surprised at her own courage | Terrified and very close to tears | Bored and ready to fall asleep | Very sorry for the poor Queen
exercise: What does Alice say when the Queen screams «Off with her head!»? | «Nonsense!» | «Please, your Majesty, I'm sorry» | «Two did it, not me» | Nothing at all; she runs
exercise: What does the King say to calm the Queen? | «Consider, my dear: she is only a child!» | «Let us all go and play croquet instead, my dear» | «Off with the gardeners!» | «She is my guest»
exercise: How does the Knave turn the gardeners over? | Very carefully, with one foot | With his hands, gently | With a flamingo | With the crown
exercise: Why does the Queen say «Leave off that! You make me giddy»? | The gardeners keep bowing to everybody | The gardeners are spinning round on the grass in circles | Alice is laughing at her | The roses smell too strong
exercise: How does Alice save the three gardeners? | She puts them into a large flower-pot | She paints them red | She tells the Queen it was all her own fault, not theirs | She hides them in her pocket
exercise: «Their heads are gone, if it please your Majesty!» What is true about this answer? | The heads are «gone» out of sight, but not cut off | The soldiers really did cut the gardeners' heads off at once | The gardeners lost their heads on purpose | The heads went to the Duchess
exercise: Why is the Duchess under sentence of execution? | She boxed the Queen's ears | She stole the Queen's tarts | She threw a frying-pan at the King | She was very late for the croquet game
exercise: What are the balls, mallets and arches in the Queen's croquet? | Hedgehogs, flamingoes and soldiers | Oranges, umbrellas and gardeners | Tarts, spoons and courtiers | Mice, pigs and cards
test: Kai: «the gardeners painted the roses white cos the Queen hates red, proper job.» (proper job = well done, West Country) Put it right. | They painted white roses red, because the tree should have been red | Right: the Queen hates red, so they are painting every red rose white before she comes | They painted red roses blue for a party | They were painting the fence, not the roses
test: «Seven jogged my elbow.» — «That's right, Five! Always lay the blame on others!» What does Seven mean? | He is being sarcastic: Five always blames someone else | He really agrees that blaming others is right | He is telling Five to blame the Queen | He is saying sorry
test: What would have happened to the gardeners if Alice had not hidden them? | They would have been led off by the three soldiers to be executed | The Queen would have given each of them a prize for painting the roses so very well | The roses would have turned red by magic | Nothing at all, as the Queen never notices anything
test: Mia: «yeah nah, Alice lay flat on her face like the gardeners.» (yeah nah = no) Is that right? | Right, it's a «no»: Alice stood still and waited | Yes, she lay flat because she was scared of the Queen | Yes, she lay down but kept one eye open | No, she ran off back to the tea-party
test: Why does Alice dare to say «Nonsense!» to the Queen? | She has realised they're only a pack of cards and she needn't be afraid | She knows the King is her uncle and will always protect her from the Queen's temper | She did not hear what the Queen said | The Cheshire Cat whispered it to her
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Queen knows the three gardeners at once.» | False: they lie face down, and their backs look like all the others' | True: she calls each of them by name the moment she sees them | True: she knows them by their red paint | False: she thinks they are three hedgehogs
test: «She boxed the Queen's ears—» Alice gives «a little scream of laughter». Why does the Rabbit hush her? | He is afraid the Queen will hear and be angry | He wants to tell the joke himself | Laughing is against the rules of croquet in the garden | The Duchess is standing right behind them
test: «Did you say 'What a pity!'?» asks the Rabbit. What did Alice really say, and what does she think? | She said «What for?» and thinks it is not at all a pity | She said «What a pity!» and feels very sorry for the Duchess indeed | She said nothing, as she was too scared to speak to the Rabbit | She said «Off with her head!» to tease him
test: The King says «she is only a child!» What does this show about him? | He is gentler and more timid than the Queen | He is the one who really makes all the rules | He wants Alice beheaded more than anyone else | He thinks Alice is just another playing card
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, the Frog-Footman received a letter. What was it? | An invitation from the Queen for the Duchess to play croquet | A bill for the Duchess's pepper | A letter from the White Rabbit to Alice, asking for his gloves and fan back | A poem about the Knave
test: Looking back: what did the White Rabbit fear, in chapter IV, when he lost his gloves and fan? | That the Duchess would get him executed, «as sure as ferrets are ferrets» | That the Queen would make him paint the roses in her garden red | That Alice would eat him | That Dinah would catch him
test: Looking back: in chapter I, Alice once tried to box her own ears. Why? | For cheating herself in a game of croquet she was playing against herself | For crying so much in the long hall that she made a pool of tears | For forgetting the key | For being rude to her sister
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, the Cheshire Cat asked Alice if she played croquet with the Queen. What did she answer? | She would like it very much, but she hadn't been invited yet | She had played it with the Queen the day before and had won every single game | She did not know what croquet was | She was afraid of the Queen
test: Looking back: what did the Cat say just before it vanished in chapter VI? | «You'll see me there» | «Off with her head!» | «Keep your temper» | «Who are you?»
test: Lily: «Alice's been scared of everyone since the start, it's giving coward.» (it's giving = it looks like) Put it right using this part. | She stands up to the Queen and says «Nonsense!» loudly — she is getting braver | Right: she hides in the flower-pot with the gardeners | Right: she lies flat on her face and shakes | She cries and runs to the White Rabbit
test: ★★★ Count the procession: ten soldiers, ten courtiers, ten royal children, then the Knave, and the King and Queen. Not counting the guests, how many is that? | 33 | 30 | 32 | 23
test: ★★★ The three soldiers stay behind to execute the gardeners. If the procession began with ten soldiers, how many soldiers walk on with it? | 7 | 10 | 13 | 3
test: ★★★ The gardeners are Two, Five and Seven. Add their numbers and take away the number of soldiers who stay behind. What do you get? | 11 | 14 | 17 | 4
exam: Identify the reason for which the gardeners were engaged in the painting of the roses. | A white rose-tree had been planted in error in place of a red one | The Queen had expressed, on the preceding day, a decided preference for roses of a crimson hue | White paint had been exhausted in the royal stores | The Knave had instructed them to disguise stolen tarts
exam: It is given that the Queen commanded the beheading of Alice. Select the response which most accurately characterises Alice's rejoinder. | She declared it «Nonsense!» loudly and decidedly | She prostrated herself upon the ground | She begged the King for a pardon | She fled in the direction of the tea-party
exam: Ascertain the means by which Alice preserved the three gardeners from execution. | Their concealment within a large flower-pot | Their removal to the Duchess's kitchen | The payment, from her own pocket, of a fine on their behalf to the Queen | An appeal to the Cheshire Cat
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the implements of the Queen's croquet? | Hedgehogs served as balls, flamingoes as mallets and soldiers as arches | Balls of wood and mallets of iron were employed, after the customary manner of that game | The arches were formed by roses painted red | The players took turns in strict order
